As you can see, only `NodeAffinity`, `NodeName`, `NodeUnschedulable`, `TaintToleration` has more than 10k data points, and others only has very few. All 10k Pods with DRA claims are scheduled successfully, so AFAIK, all pods should be filtered by `DynamicResource` plugins. so, the count of doing`FilterPlugin` of `DynamicResource` should be far more greater than 10k. but now it's not and it's really confused me.

I aware that there is a sampling mechanism for `scheduler_plugin_execution_duration_seconds` and the sample percent is **10%**. But it's still can not explain why, since the sampling is per pod schedling cycle, and 10k successful scheduling should lead at least 10k times filter by `DynamicResource`. even with the sampling, the count should be around 1k, but now it's **36**.

After some digging work, I assume that it caused by `MetricAsyncRecorder`, see https://github.com/kubernetes/kubernetes/blob/b5ed15b94ab11acf1f89822df6a04013c3335008/pkg/scheduler/metrics/metric_recorder.go#L208-L224
Scheduler runs `FlushMetrics` in every **one second**,  and the buffer size of channel is **1k**. So when scheduler runs in a large cluster, and the metric of currently scheduling cycle is enabled, every filter and scoring plugins will create a metric event for **each Pod on each Node**. All those metrics will be sent to the channel, and the channel will be full quickly, cause event from plugin placed in later position are dropped. The later a plugin is placed, the more likely to l drop its event.

I aware that there is performance concern, but current is not ideal either. some plugins place in later position, and I can barely monitor it via metrics.

### What did you expect to happen?

Every scheduling plugins can have even chance to be observed via metrics.

